Z||000002||Boomtastic||11-03-2001||05:43 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Haze_Performance:<BR><B><I><P>[b]Has anyone ever seen this?</B></I>[/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I have dealt with him. We collaborated on a project. He got to use my '88 lower control arms to build prototype bushings, and I got a set when he was done. They are very well engineered.<P>Unless you're talking about your signature line, in which case I've seen that as well .. (ahem)<P>------------------<BR>Boomtastic<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/mirror_md_clr.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Mach10||11-03-2001||03:50 PM||||What are those things made of? Looks like the plastic they use to make seeing-eye-cane tips ? If it is, then it ALWAYS feels "greasy" and I can't see it EVER squeeking.||||reg||5
Z||000004||Boomtastic||11-03-2001||04:08 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Mach10:<BR><B>What are those things made of? Looks like the plastic they use to make seeing-eye-cane tips ? If it is, then it ALWAYS feels "greasy" and I can't see it EVER squeeking.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The next blind guy I see walking by I'll wrestle him down and check out his cane.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> Actually, they feel just like plastic .. VERY hard, solid plastic. They are machined on all surfaces except the ends. They use the metal sleeve that is in the rubber bushing through the middle and self-tapping screws to keep the bushing stationary.<P>------------------<BR>Boomtastic<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/mirror_md_clr.gif"> ||||reg||1
